{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head ec0261df9c5f782aaadc9a921273d84f2247d9cf on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering bc311b36 under my promise c690649c. This is the implementation report; no separate readiness report follows.\n\nWHAT IS THERE. checkpointEventCache now accounts the bytes it retains — compressed chunks plus the raw tail — against the same 256 MiB limit the marshal already enforced, so an oversized history fails while it accumulates rather than after the memory is spent. Each chunk is built through the checkpointLimitWriter that already existed in this file, with the remaining budget as its limit, so an in-progress chunk cannot pass the budget either; that closes the window a completed-chunk check alone would leave open. A new fail() records the first error and drops chunks, tail and both counters at once, so the memory is reclaimed instead of held behind the error, and every error path in the cache now goes through it rather than only the overflow.\n\nBoth accumulation sites are covered because ordinary appends and streamed full rebuilds feed the same methods. The streamed sink now distinguishes an oversized cache from a real stream failure, so an overflow fails only the checkpoint and the verified read still completes.\n\nTHE ONE SEMANTIC CHANGE, AND WHY IT IS THE POINT. writeCheckpointCache used to flatten any cache error into ErrNoUsableCheckpoint. It now returns the cache error itself. I traced this rather than take it on faith: recordCheckpointFailure at kernel.go:934 tests errors.Is(err, errCheckpointTooLarge) and only then sets checkpointOversized, and checkpointLimitWriter wraps exactly that sentinel. So a byte overflow now reaches the terminal oversized latch that a marshal-time overflow already reached, which is what your conditions asked for; under the old flattening it could never have. Both call sites, at kernel.go:877 and :926, feed recordCheckpointFailure, so nothing downstream was comparing the old sentinel. A verified full rebuild remains the single reset boundary and no retry behaviour is added.\n\nCompact checkpoint v3 framing, streamed rebuild structure, eventWithoutPayload dedup and the Git replacement quarantine are untouched, and none of the legacy JSON retention or preflight work from ff999f86 or 49156978 came back.\n\nTESTS, AND WHAT KILLS THEM. Seven tests in internal/kernel/checkpoint_cache_test.go exercise the bound at small size through an unexported limit field that only tests set; one of them pins the production default to the marshal constant, so the seam cannot hide a wrong default. Four mutations, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile before the tests ran:\n\n  Removing the accounting: six tests fail.\n  Unbounding only the streamed path: the streamed-rebuild test alone fails, which is the test that exists because fixing one accumulation site and missing its twin is the obvious way to get this wrong.\n  Keeping the error but skipping the release: three fail. Two others pass legitimately, because in those cases nothing had been retained before the overflow, so their release assertions are vacuous. Reported rather than counted as kills.\n  Raising the bound: all seven fail, including the default-pinning one.\n\nGATES ACTUALLY RUN, by me, at this exact head with a clean tree. gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... clean, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 across all thirty packages, internal/kernel at 188s and cmd/gs at 181s.\n\nTHINGS YOU SHOULD WEIGH, NOT DISCOVER. The head was cut from f4281e80 and I rebased it onto current main a7cf9b60 before publishing, so this describes work on current main; the gates above were run after the rebase, not before. The tail accounting counts payload and attachment bytes, the memory actually retained, and not the roughly twelve bytes per event of compact framing, so the bound slightly under-counts framed size; the marshal limit still guards the published artifact exactly. During a tail flush the transient peak can reach the retained budget plus one compressed chunk before the tail is released, and each term is individually within the limit.\n\nTwo process facts about how this was produced, because they bear on how much the mutation table is worth. One mutation run was lost to machine sleep and produced no output; it was rerun in the foreground and the table above comes only from complete runs. At one point the uncommitted working change was destroyed by a stray checkout and reconstructed; that is why I read the entire committed diff myself rather than accepting a summary of it.","body":{"commit":"ec0261df9c5f782aaadc9a921273d84f2247d9cf","path":"internal/kernel"}}